// Rejected at resolve (typing): a lexicographic tuple key is ADR 0031
// Decision 7's tier-2 tie spelling, and it is **not yet supported**.
//
// A value tuple has no type in the checker today (the tuple syntax exists only
// to destructure a tupled lambda's parameters, ADR 0030 Decision 2), and adding
// one collides with that convention: `f (a, b)` currently binds two parameters
// rather than passing one pair.  That is an open design question, so the
// diagnostic names the gap instead of reporting a generic "unsupported".
//
// Scalar keys cover every binding in the bundled `series` module, so nothing in
// the shipped vocabulary waits on this.
//
// **When tuple keys land, the injectivity rule must extend with them.**  A scan
// requires a tie-free order key, discharged either by a grading over
// `key + {column}` (`Mensura.keyInjOn_demote_tag`) or by an explicit claim on
// the pipeline.  A tuple key is injective when the *whole component set* is,
// which is a grading over `key + {c1, c2, ...}`, so the lookup generalizes from
// one column to a set rather than needing a new mechanism.  Two things to get
// right at that point:
//
//   - the components' union is what must be graded, not each component
//     separately (a tuple can be injective when no single part is, which is the
//     entire reason tier 2 exists);
//   - a `desc` marker on any component is transparent to the question, since
//     the dual of an injective key is injective.
//
// Whoever implements tier 2 should extend the grading lookup and the claim in
// the same change, or a tuple key will silently bypass a check its scalar
// counterpart has to pass.
unit Machine {
  id: string
}

store readings {
  unit { Machine }
  attr* {
    temperature: real
    taken_at: date
    seq: int
  }
}

view bad {
  readings
    |> map_bags |k, b| (
      .run = scan `+` (|r| r.temperature) (|r| (r.taken_at, r.seq)) b
    )
}
